Chapter: Console

 We say:

Section: Definitions

  effect Console where
    print-line-uni : Text -> [Console.Write] Nothing
    read-line  : [Console.Read] Text

Page 1

Chapter: DeviceMath

 We say:

Section: Clamps and Selection

  real-min : Real, Real -> Real
  real-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  real-max : Real, Real -> Real
  real-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  real-abs : Real -> Real
  real-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

Section: Square Root

  real-sqrt : Real -> Real
  real-sqrt (x) =
    if x <= 0.0 then 0.0
    else dm-sqrt-scaled x 1.0 700

  dm-sqrt-scaled : Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  dm-sqrt-scaled (x) (s) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then s * dm-sqrt-core x
    else if x >= 4.0 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x / 4.0) (s * 2.0) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.25 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x * 4.0) (s * 0.5) (fuel - 1)
    else s * dm-sqrt-core x

  dm-sqrt-core : Real -> Real
  dm-sqrt-core (r) =
    let g0 = (r + 1.0) * 0.5
    in let g1 = (g0 + r / g0) * 0.5
    in let g2 = (g1 + r / g1) * 0.5
    in let g3 = (g2 + r / g2) * 0.5
    in let g4 = (g3 + r / g3) * 0.5
    in (g4 + r / g4) * 0.5

Section: Sine and Cosine

  dm-two-pi : Real
  dm-two-pi = 6.283185307179586

  dm-pi : Real
  dm-pi = 3.141592653589793

  dm-half-pi : Real
  dm-half-pi = 1.570796326794897

  dm-reduce : Real -> Real
  dm-reduce (x) =
    let k = real-from-int (real-to-int (x / dm-two-pi))
    in let r = x - k * dm-two-pi
    in if r > dm-pi then r - dm-two-pi
       else if r < 0.0 - dm-pi then r + dm-two-pi
       else r

  dm-fold-quadrant : Real -> Real
  dm-fold-quadrant (r) =
    if r > dm-half-pi then dm-pi - r
    else if r < 0.0 - dm-half-pi then 0.0 - dm-pi - r
    else r

  dm-sin-poly : Real -> Real
  dm-sin-poly (r) =
    let r2 = r * r
    in let r3 = r2 * r
    in let r5 = r3 * r2
    in let r7 = r5 * r2
    in let r9 = r7 * r2
    in let r11 = r9 * r2
    in r - r3 / 6.0 + r5 / 120.0 - r7 / 5040.0 + r9 / 362880.0 - r11 / 39916800.0

  real-sin : Real -> Real
  real-sin (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce x))

  real-cos : Real -> Real
  real-cos (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce (x + dm-half-pi)))

Section: Arc Tangent

 The inverse of the pair above, and the one this chapter was missing. Nothing in
 the foreword answered an arc tangent at Real: Math chapter Cordic and Math
 chapter Geodesic both have one, and both are integer milliradian routines whose
 output cannot be fed back into real-sin.

 THE ARGUMENT IS HALVED BEFORE THE SERIES, WHICH IS THE WHOLE METHOD. The Taylor
 series for the arc tangent converges hopelessly near |t| = 1 -- that is where
 its terms stop shrinking -- and |t| near 1 is exactly what a caller taking the
 angle of a diagonal hands it. The half-angle identity

     atan t = 2 * atan (t / (1 + sqrt (1 + t*t)))

 shrinks the argument geometrically instead. Four applications take |t| <= 1
 down to 0.0492: the angle is at most pi/4, so a sixteenth of it is at most
 0.0490874 and its tangent is 0.0491258. Six terms there are good to 1.1e-17,
 and the sixteen the result is multiplied back by cannot undo that.

 Above |t| = 1 the reciprocal identity folds the argument back into range first,
 so the series never sees a value it handles badly.

 ACCURACY: worst absolute error 6.7e-16 radians, 4 ULP, against zig 0.16.0
 std.math.atan. The series truncation above is not what bounds it. dm-half-pi
 parses 2 ulp above pi/2, an error of 4.4e-16, and that flows into every
 |t| > 1 result and into real-atan2's x = 0 arms, so the constant dominates the
 method by five orders.

  dm-atan-half-step : Real -> Real
  dm-atan-half-step (t) = t / (1.0 + real-sqrt (1.0 + t * t))

  dm-atan-halve : Real, Integer -> Real
  dm-atan-halve (t) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then t
    else dm-atan-halve (dm-atan-half-step t) (n - 1)

  dm-atan-series : Real -> Real
  dm-atan-series (u) =
    let u2 = u * u
    in let u3 = u2 * u
    in let u5 = u3 * u2
    in let u7 = u5 * u2
    in let u9 = u7 * u2
    in let u11 = u9 * u2
    in u - u3 / 3.0 + u5 / 5.0 - u7 / 7.0 + u9 / 9.0 - u11 / 11.0

 Four halvings, so the series result is scaled back by two to the fourth.

  dm-atan-core : Real -> Real
  dm-atan-core (t) = 16.0 * dm-atan-series (dm-atan-halve t 4)

  real-atan : Real -> Real
  real-atan (t) =
    if t > 1.0 then dm-half-pi - dm-atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else if t < 0.0 - 1.0 then (0.0 - dm-half-pi) - dm-atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else dm-atan-core t

 A caller that divides its two coordinates and calls real-atan has folded two
 quadrants onto one and cannot get them back; real-atan2 branches before it
 divides.

 The x = 0 arms are exact rather than a limit, and the y = 0, x = 0 case answers
 zero, which is what a caller comparing against zig's std.math.atan2 will expect
 of two positive zeroes. It diverges from that reference on a NEGATIVE zero:
 -0.0 >= 0.0 is true, so real-atan2 (-0.0) (-1.0) answers +pi where zig answers
 -pi, and real-atan2 (-0.0) (1.0) answers +0.0 where zig answers -0.0.

  real-atan2 : Real, Real -> Real
  real-atan2 (y) (x) =
    if x > 0.0 then real-atan (y / x)
    else if x < 0.0 then (if y >= 0.0 then real-atan (y / x) + dm-pi else real-atan (y / x) - dm-pi)
    else if y > 0.0 then dm-half-pi
    else if y < 0.0 then 0.0 - dm-half-pi
    else 0.0

Chapter: Trig
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath

 Sine, cosine, tangent and arc tangent in Real.

 THE STAND-IN IS RETIRED. This chapter used to carry its own sine and cosine --
 the same reduction, quadrant fold and 11th-order Taylor polynomial DeviceMath
 has -- because DeviceMath's dm-reduce calls real-from-int and the zig plug had no
 emitter for it. That emitter landed (PLUG_WORK, 2026-08-29), so `r-sin` and
 `r-cos` are now DeviceMath's, and the duplicate implementation is gone.

 THREE CONSTANTS SURVIVED THAT CLEANUP AND SHOULD NOT HAVE. `pi`, `two-pi` and
 `half-pi` were written out here digit for digit while `dm-pi`, `dm-two-pi` and
 `dm-half-pi` sat in the chapter this one already cites, identical to the last
 place. They are aliases now. A retired implementation with its constants left
 behind is the easy half of de-duplicating, and it is worth naming because the
 prose above had already claimed the job was done.

 THE NAMES STAY AND THE IMPLEMENTATION DOES NOT, which is the whole change. Nine
 chapters call `r-sin`, `r-cos` or `r-tan`, and none of them should have to know
 whether the sine underneath is the foreword's or the port's -- that was the point
 of the names. What they buy now is one place to look and one place that already
 cites DeviceMath, rather than nine new cites for a rename.

 RETIRING IT MOVED NUMBERS, and that was the reason it waited rather than being
 tidied away with the rest. The old `wrap` subtracted two-pi in a bounded loop;
 dm-reduce divides and converts back through the integers, so the two disagree in
 the last bits and every angle in the game runs through one of them. See the
 tolerance note in README and D10.

 We say:

Section: Constants

  pi : Real
  pi = dm-pi

  two-pi : Real
  two-pi = dm-two-pi

  half-pi : Real
  half-pi = dm-half-pi

  deg : Real
  deg = 0.017453292519943295

Section: Angle Wrapping

  wrap : Real, Integer -> Real
  wrap (x) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then x
    else if x > pi then wrap (x - two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.0 - pi then wrap (x + two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else x

Section: Sine and Cosine

 `wrap` OUTLIVED THE SINE IT WAS WRITTEN FOR, and it turns out not to have been
 part of the stand-in at all. mountains.zig and sky.zig each carry their own
 `wrap` -- normalising a bearing difference into the half-open turn around zero --
 and both are ported through this one. It reads as an angle utility now rather
 than as an argument reduction, which is what those two callers always wanted;
 what it is NOT any more is anything sine depends on.

 The fuel is the zig's own shape: mountains.zig's wrap is a bounded `while`, not a
 modulo, for exactly the same reason.

  r-sin : Real -> Real
  r-sin (x) = real-sin x

  r-cos : Real -> Real
  r-cos (x) = real-cos x

 DeviceMath has no tangent, so this one is still the port's -- and it is the
 obvious quotient rather than anything clever, exactly as it was.

  r-tan : Real -> Real
  r-tan (x) = real-sin x / real-cos x

Section: Arc Tangent

 THIS ONE IS NOT A STAND-IN. `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` has no atan at all -- its
 whole surface is min, max, abs, sqrt, sin and cos -- and the foreword's other
 arc tangents (Math chapter Cordic, Math chapter Geodesic) are INTEGER milli-unit
 routines, one of which is recorded in PORTING_NOTES A4 as four times less
 accurate than it claims. So a Real atan is a gap in Codex's foreword rather than
 a hole in the zig plug, and it does not go away when the rest of this chapter is
 retired in favour of DeviceMath.

 The method is argument HALVING, not a Taylor series applied directly: the series
 for atan converges hopelessly slowly near |t| = 1, which is exactly where the
 mountains sample it. The half-angle identity

     atan t = 2 * atan (t / (1 + sqrt (1 + t*t)))

 shrinks the argument geometrically, so four applications take |t| <= 1 down to
 about 0.06, where a six-term series is good to roughly 1e-14 and the sixteen it
 is multiplied back by cannot undo that. Above |t| = 1 the reciprocal identity
 folds it back into range first; the screen's outer columns at a pulled-in focal
 do reach |t| near 3, so that branch is live and not defensive.

  atan-half-step : Real -> Real
  atan-half-step (t) = t / (1.0 + real-sqrt (1.0 + t * t))

  atan-halve : Real, Integer -> Real
  atan-halve (t) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then t
    else atan-halve (atan-half-step t) (n - 1)

  atan-series : Real -> Real
  atan-series (u) =
    let u2 = u * u
    in let u3 = u2 * u
    in let u5 = u3 * u2
    in let u7 = u5 * u2
    in let u9 = u7 * u2
    in let u11 = u9 * u2
    in u - u3 / 3.0 + u5 / 5.0 - u7 / 7.0 + u9 / 9.0 - u11 / 11.0

 Four halvings, so the series result is scaled back by 2^4.

  atan-core : Real -> Real
  atan-core (t) = 16.0 * atan-series (atan-halve t 4)

  r-atan : Real -> Real
  r-atan (t) =
    if t > 1.0 then half-pi - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else if t < 0.0 - 1.0 then (0.0 - half-pi) - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else atan-core t

 The TWO-argument arc tangent, which is not a convenience wrapper: it is the one
 that knows which quadrant the point is in, and gaze.zig needs that because the
 pig it is aiming at can be behind the rider as well as ahead. Dividing first and
 calling r-atan would fold two quadrants onto one.

 The x = 0 arms are exact rather than a limit, and the y = 0, x = 0 case answers
 zero, which is what std.math.atan2 does with two positive zeros.

  r-atan2 : Real, Real -> Real
  r-atan2 (y) (x) =
    if x > 0.0 then r-atan (y / x)
    else if x < 0.0 then (if y >= 0.0 then r-atan (y / x) + pi else r-atan (y / x) - pi)
    else if y > 0.0 then half-pi
    else if y < 0.0 then 0.0 - half-pi
    else 0.0

 Zig's std.math.sign, which answers zero AT zero rather than picking a side.

  r-sign : Real -> Real
  r-sign (x) = if x > 0.0 then 1.0 else (if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else 0.0)

Chapter: Geom
  cites Safari chapter Trig

 Pure rider-relative geometry: the coordinate types, the world-to-rider transform,
 the ground curvature, and near-plane clipping. No projection -- that is Camera --
 no drawing, no allocation. The math leaf the rest of the camera stands on.

 A port of wasm/geom.zig.

 We say:

Section: Coordinates

 A ground-plane point measured FROM THE RIDER: how far forward, down his look
 axis, and how far to his right. The fundamental coordinate of the whole scene.

  RiderPt = record {
    right : Real,
    forward : Real
  }

 A rider-relative vertex carrying a height off the ground -- what the road quads
 and raised structures clip and project as.

  Vec3 = record {
    right : Real,
    forward : Real,
    height : Real
  }

 A point in a segment's bottom-left frame: `a` along, `x` across-from-left.

  AX = record {
    a : Real,
    x : Real
  }

Section: Ground

 The local ground is a gentle spherical plateau: a point drops this far below the
 rider's tangent plane at horizontal distance d, so the road bends toward a finite
 horizon instead of a vanishing point at infinity. Larger radius = gentler.

  ground-radius : Real
  ground-radius = 100000.0

  ground-drop : Real, Real -> Real
  ground-drop (right) (forward) = (right * right + forward * forward) / (2.0 * ground-radius)

Section: The Rider Transform

 to-rider maps a point in a segment's bottom-left frame -- `a` along the segment,
 `x` across from its LEFT edge -- into the rider's frame, given his pose along and
 across the segment's centre line and his look angle `yaw`. His pose is
 centre-relative, so the half-width `hw` shifts x to from-the-left.

  to-rider : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  to-rider (a) (x) (cam-along) (cam-across) (yaw) (hw) =
    let d-a = a - cam-along
    in let d-x = x - (cam-across + hw)
    in let c = r-cos yaw
    in let s = r-sin yaw
    in RiderPt { forward = d-a * c + d-x * s, right = (0.0 - d-a) * s + d-x * c }

Section: The Frame Fusion

 next-to-cur maps a point from the NEXT segment's bottom-left frame into the
 CURRENT segment's, across the join. LEFT and RIGHT are NOT mirror images: a left
 turn fuses on the end-left edge, where the origin is the inner corner; a right
 turn fuses on the end-right edge and pays a width shift. `turns-right` is the
 turn direction.

  next-to-cur : Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real -> AX
  next-to-cur (a-b) (x-b) (seg-len) (theta) (turns-right) (width) =
    let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in if turns-right
      then AX { a = a-b * c - x-b * s + width * s + seg-len, x = x-b * c + a-b * s + width * (1.0 - c) }
      else AX { a = a-b * c + x-b * s + seg-len, x = x-b * c - a-b * s }

 cur-to-next is the inverse: a point in the CURRENT segment's frame expressed in
 the NEXT segment's, across the same join. Used to draw the joint just BEHIND the
 rider, so the pavement he is still crossing does not vanish.

  cur-to-next : Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real -> AX
  cur-to-next (a) (x) (seg-len) (theta) (turns-right) (width) =
    let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in if turns-right
      then let a0 = a - seg-len - width * s
        in let x0 = x - width * (1.0 - c)
        in AX { a = a0 * c + x0 * s, x = (0.0 - a0) * s + x0 * c }
      else let a0 = a - seg-len
        in AX { a = a0 * c - x * s, x = a0 * s + x * c }

Section: Line Intersection

 line-meet returns where two infinite lines cross in the rider frame: line A
 through a0 toward a1, line B through b0 toward b1. Used for the corner's outer
 apex, where the two segments' outer shoulders meet. A real turn has a non-zero
 angle, so the lines are never parallel.

  line-meet : RiderPt, RiderPt, RiderPt, RiderPt -> RiderPt
  line-meet (a0) (a1) (b0) (b1) =
    let dax = a1.right - a0.right
    in let daf = a1.forward - a0.forward
    in let dbx = b1.right - b0.right
    in let dbf = b1.forward - b0.forward
    in let t = ((b0.right - a0.right) * dbf - (b0.forward - a0.forward) * dbx) / (dax * dbf - daf * dbx)
    in RiderPt { right = a0.right + t * dax, forward = a0.forward + t * daf }

Section: Near-Plane Clipping

 Clip a rider-frame polygon against the near plane at `near` metres forward, so no
 vertex sits behind the eye where the perspective divide would fling it across the
 screen. Fewer than 3 vertices out means the caller skips the polygon.

 The zig writes into a caller-supplied `out` slice and returns a count; this
 returns the list, which is the same thing without the buffer. The wrap-around
 edge is the reason the index walks to the length and reads (i + 1) modulo it: the
 polygon is closed, so the last vertex pairs with the first.

 `near` lived in Camera until 2026-09-03, declared under its Lens section and
 read by nothing in that chapter. Four callers carried it from there to
 `clip-near` here, into a parameter of the same name. It is the near plane and
 the near plane is this section's business.

  near : Real
  near = 0.4

  clip-cross : Vec3, Vec3, Real -> List Vec3
  clip-cross (a) (b) (near) =
    let f = (near - a.forward) / (b.forward - a.forward)
    in [Vec3 { right = a.right + f * (b.right - a.right), forward = near, height = a.height + f * (b.height - a.height) }]

  both-sides : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  both-sides (a-in) (b-in) = if a-in then b-in else (if b-in then False else True)

  clip-near-edge : List Vec3, Real, Integer -> List Vec3
  clip-near-edge (poly) (near) (i) =
    let n = list-length poly
    in if i >= n then []
    else let a = list-at poly i
    in let b = list-at poly ((i + 1) - (((i + 1) / n) * n))
    in let a-in = a.forward >= near
    in let b-in = b.forward >= near
    in let kept = if a-in then [a] else []
    in let crossed = if both-sides a-in b-in then [] else clip-cross a b near
    in kept & crossed & clip-near-edge poly near (i + 1)

  clip-near : List Vec3, Real -> List Vec3
  clip-near (poly) (near) = clip-near-edge poly near 0

Chapter: Camera
  cites Safari chapter Geom

 The perspective projection: a rider-relative ground point at a height becomes a
 screen pixel. Owns the eye height and the screen height. Owns no lens and no
 near plane. Pure; no drawing, no allocation.

 A port of wasm/camera.zig, and no longer the shape of it. On 2026-09-03 the
 lens moved to `Safari chapter Lens` and the near plane to `Safari chapter
 Geom`, because this chapter never read either: `project` is handed a focal,
 and the near plane was carried by four callers straight into Geom's clipping.
 Fidelity to the zig file boundaries is not a constraint here; agreement
 between the arms is, and it is untouched by which chapter a definition sits in.

 We say:

Section: Projection

  camera-h : Real
  camera-h = 600.0

  eye-h : Real
  eye-h = 1.2

  ScreenPt = record {
    x : Real,
    y : Real
  }

 The perspective divide is by forward, so callers must clip to the near plane
 first -- forward is then never near zero. `cf` is the live camera focal, which
 the caller gets from `cam-focal`; passing it rather than reading it is what
 keeps this chapter independent of the lens.

  project : Vec3, Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  project (p) (cf) (view-w) =
    ScreenPt {
      x = view-w / 2.0 + (p.right / p.forward) * cf,
      y = camera-h / 2.0 - ((p.height - eye-h) / p.forward) * cf
    }

 The list form. It lived in `Safari chapter GuardRail` until 2026-09-03, which
 is where the first caller happened to be; Render and TruckDraw read it from
 there too, so a rail chapter was on the cite line of everything that projects
 a polygon. It is `project` in a loop and it belongs beside it.

  project-all : List Vec3, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  project-all (ps) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [project (list-at ps i) cf view-w] & project-all ps cf view-w (i + 1)

Chapter: Paint
  cites Safari chapter Camera

 The seam to JS: a draw command in wasm/paint.zig's wire.

 The zig writes each command into a fixed pre-sized buffer in linear memory and
 hands the browser a byte length. The port is pure, so it RETURNS commands and the
 wasm shim does the writing -- the same split NOTES calls seam 3, and the reason
 one grader serves every module: a frame is a flat list either way.

 The wire:

   tag 0   [0][color][nPoints][x f32][y f32]...
   tag 1   [1][color][strength f32][nPoints][x f32][y f32]...
   tag 3   [3][color][x f32][y f32][r f32][alpha f32]
   tag 4   [4][rgbaCentre][rgbaEdge][cx][cy][r][nPoints][x][y]...
   tag 5   [5][rgba0][rgba1][off0][off1][ax][ay][bx][by][nPoints][x][y]...
   tag 6   [6][rgba0][rgba1][off0][off1][cx][cy][ux][uy][vx][vy][nPoints][x][y]...

 TAG 2 IS NOT HERE AND IS ON THE WIRE. This chapter emits what `wasm/paint.zig`
 emits and nothing else, because that is what nine checks in judge/ grade. The
 shim runs `Safari chapter Blit`'s `blit-expand` over the finished list before
 writing it, and that pass turns every tag 1 into a tag 0 or a

   tag 2   [2][edgeColor][middleColor][x0 f32][x1 f32][nPoints][x f32][y f32]...

 -- a polygon under a symmetric three-stop gradient across [x0, x1], colours
 already computed. So tag 1 is what this chapter produces and tag 2 is what the
 browser receives, and no reader of this file should go looking for a tag 1 in
 `web/blitter.js`.

 tag 0 is a solid fill; tag 1 is a horizontal round gradient -- dark edge, bright
 centre, dark edge across the polygon's x-extent -- with a per-polygon strength
 that lets crown shading fade to flat with distance instead of popping. strength
 rides on every command so the record has one shape; tag 0 ignores it, exactly as
 the blitter does.

 Coordinates are SCREEN pixels here and still Real. They narrow to f32 in the
 shim, at the buffer, which is the only place the game's width is load-bearing.

 We say:

Section: Commands

  DrawCmd = record {
    tag : Integer,
    color : Integer,
    color2 : Integer,
    strength : Real,
    geom : List Real,
    pts : List Real
  }

 TWO FIELDS THE GRADIENT TAGS FORCED, and taking them changed the record's rule
 for the better. `pts` is now ALWAYS the polygon and `geom` is always the
 command's own parameters -- a beacon's centre and radius, a gradient's stops and
 axes. Before, the beacon smuggled its x, y and r through `pts`, which meant `pts`
 sometimes held points and sometimes did not, and every reader had to know which.
 Now one rule covers every tag.

 `color2` is the second stop. Tags 0, 1 and 3 leave it zero and leave `geom` empty
 or short; nothing reads a field its tag does not define.

  flatten-screen : List ScreenPt, Integer -> List Real
  flatten-screen (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else let p = list-at ps i
    in [p.x, p.y] & flatten-screen ps (i + 1)

  push-poly : Integer, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-poly (color) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 0, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = 0.0, geom = [], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

 pushRoundPoly drops a polygon with fewer than three points rather than emitting
 a degenerate one; push-poly is given the same guard so the two agree.

  push-round-poly : Integer, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-round-poly (color) (strength) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 1, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = strength, geom = [], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

 THE BEACON IS NOT A POLYGON, and it is the first command here that is not. It is
 a disc -- a centre, a radius and an alpha -- and the blitter draws a true arc for
 it. It gets its own tag because unlike every other command it is NOT OPAQUE, and
 the colour word on the solid path carries no alpha to say so.

 It still fits the one DrawCmd shape, which is the point of that shape. The
 record's prose already argues that strength rides on every command so there is
 one record rather than two; the same argument carries the disc. `strength` is the
 alpha the blitter composites at, and `pts` holds x, y and r -- three Reals where a
 polygon holds an even number of them. The shim branches on the tag when it
 writes, exactly as paint.zig's own pushBeacon does.

  push-beacon : Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  push-beacon (color) (x) (y) (r) (alpha) =
    [DrawCmd { tag = 3, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = alpha, geom = [x, y, r], pts = [] }]

Section: The Gradient Fills

 Three more tags, all polygons with a gradient instead of a flat colour, and all
 three carry TWO colours in 0xAARRGGBB rather than the 0xRRGGBB the solid path
 uses -- because these composite, and the alpha is what makes a headlight beam a
 beam rather than a white wedge.

 Tag 4 is a RADIAL fill from a centre out to a radius: the truck's headlight beams
 and their glow. Tags 5 and 6 are 2-stop gradients with explicit stop offsets --
 5 linear along an axis, 6 radial through an ELLIPSE given by two axis vectors --
 and they are the bull's shading panels, the one animal whose art is the Fluent
 Color variant rather than the Flat one.

 All three drop a polygon with fewer than three points, exactly as the solid path
 does and for the same reason.

  push-grad-poly : Integer, Integer, Real, Real, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-grad-poly (rgba-center) (rgba-edge) (cx) (cy) (r) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 4, color = rgba-center, color2 = rgba-edge, strength = 0.0, geom = [cx, cy, r], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

  push-linear-grad-poly : Integer, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-linear-grad-poly (rgba0) (rgba1) (off0) (off1) (ax) (ay) (bx) (by) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 5, color = rgba0, color2 = rgba1, strength = 0.0, geom = [off0, off1, ax, ay, bx, by], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

  push-radial-grad-poly : Integer, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-radial-grad-poly (rgba0) (rgba1) (off0) (off1) (cx) (cy) (ux) (uy) (vx) (vy) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 6, color = rgba0, color2 = rgba1, strength = 0.0, geom = [off0, off1, cx, cy, ux, uy, vx, vy], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

Chapter: Num

 The Real primitives Codex's foreword does not have.

 `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` is the only Real chapter in the foreword and its whole
 surface is min, max, abs, sqrt, sin and cos. There is no rounding, no floor and
 no modulo, and the foreword's other numeric chapters -- Cordic, Easing, Color,
 GameCamera, Numeric -- are all INTEGER milli-units, so they answer a different
 question. Everything here is a gap upstream rather than a hole in the zig plug,
 and unlike `Safari chapter Trig` none of it is a stand-in for something that
 already exists.

 EVERY FUNCTION HERE NEEDS real-to-int, so none of it could have been written
 before 2026-08-29 -- see plugs-backlog row 2.03. That is the honest reason
 this chapter did not exist earlier: the port had no way to reach the integers
 at all.

 We say:

Section: Rounding

 Zig's @round goes to nearest and ties AWAY FROM ZERO, which truncation does not
 give. TRUNCATE FIRST AND THEN LOOK AT WHAT IS LEFT: real-to-int already truncates
 toward zero, `x - t` is exact for every x this converter accepts, and a leftover
 of half or more in either direction is precisely the away-from-zero tie. One rule
 covers both signs, which is why there is no sign branch here any more.

 ADDING THE HALF FIRST IS THE VERSION THAT WAS WRONG, and NumCheck is what found
 it. `real-to-int (x + 0.5)` answers 1 for x = 0.49999999999999994 -- the largest
 double below a half -- because x + 0.5 is not representable and rounds UP to
 exactly 1.0, and the mirrored form answered -1 at -0.49999999999999994 where zig
 answers -0. Every caller in the game rounds a 0..255 colour channel, a stride
 count, a step count or a whole number of degrees, so not one of them could see
 it; a round that is wrong on a value none of today's callers produces is a trap
 for the next caller, and that is the whole reason this chapter is graded on a
 domain wider than its callers'.

  round-real : Real -> Real
  round-real (x) =
    let t = real-from-int (real-to-int x)
    in let f = x - t
    in if f >= 0.5 then t + 1.0 else if f <= 0.0 - 0.5 then t - 1.0 else t

 real-to-int truncates toward zero, so for a negative non-integral value it lands
 ABOVE its argument and floor is one less. An exact integer is its own floor in
 both arms, which is why the test is `>` and not `>=`.

  floor-real : Real -> Real
  floor-real (x) =
    let t = real-from-int (real-to-int x)
    in if t > x then t - 1.0 else t

Section: Modulo

 Zig's @mod is the FLOORED modulo -- its result takes the sign of the divisor --
 and that is not the truncated remainder. Floored is x - m * floor (x / m), and
 this is the faithful reading of what tower.zig asks for.

 BUT THE ONE CALLER CANNOT TELL THE DIFFERENCE, and that is worth stating rather
 than implying coverage this does not have. tower.zig writes

     @mod(@mod(phase, P) + P, P)

 which looks like belt and braces over an operator that is already non-negative,
 and is in fact exactly what makes the two semantics AGREE. Floored gives m in
 [0, P) directly and the second pass is a no-op. Truncated gives r in (-P, P);
 adding P lands in (0, 2P); the second pass takes P off again when it overflowed
 and leaves it alone when it did not -- and r + P is precisely floored's answer
 whenever r was negative. So the double form is semantics-agnostic by
 construction.

 MEASURED, not reasoned about after the fact: replacing this with the truncated
 remainder leaves TowerCheck entirely green, including its negative-phase case.
 An earlier draft of this note claimed tower.zig "relies on" the floored form and
 that the two "disagree there". Both were wrong, and the red test said so.

 Floored stays because it is what @mod means and the next caller may not wrap its
 argument twice. But nothing in this port grades the choice today.

  mod-real : Real, Real -> Real
  mod-real (x) (m) = x - m * floor-real (x / m)

Section: The Exponential

 rider.zig's shoulder brake decays as exp(-n / 20) over up to 2000 frames, so the
 argument reaches -100 and a bare Taylor series is useless there. The standard
 reduction: take k as the nearest integer to x / ln 2, so the remainder r is
 within half a ln 2 of zero, sum the series there, and scale by 2^k.

 THIS NEEDS real-to-int TWICE OVER -- once to round k, once to count the doublings
 -- so like everything else here it could not have been written before the
 conversion emitters landed.

  ln2 : Real
  ln2 = 0.6931471805599453

 TWELVE TERMS OVER |r| <= 0.347 LEAVE A REMAINDER OF 1.7e-16, NOT THE 1e-17 THIS
 NOTE CLAIMED BEFORE IT WAS MEASURED. r^13/13! at r = ln2/2 is 1.671e-16, which is
 an arithmetic fact nobody had worked out. NumCheck now measures the whole chapter
 end to end against zig's @exp over eighteen arguments: the worst RELATIVE error is
 8.5e-16, at x = 20, and the gate is 1e-15 because the stream reddens at 8e-16.

 AND IT IS THE REDUCTION, NOT THE SERIES, THAT SETS THAT NUMBER. The three
 arguments small enough to need no reduction at all -- -0.05, 0 and 0.05 -- come
 out EXACT; every nonzero error in the table belongs to a reduced case. Taking
 k * ln2 off x cancels, and `ln2` here is one f64 where a library keeps a hi/lo
 pair for exactly this reason. Splitting it is the fix if it ever matters:
 rider.zig consumes the answer in f32, where 8.5e-16 is nine orders below the last
 bit it can hold, so today it does not.

 WRITING A BOUND NOBODY CHECKED IS THE DEFECT THIS PORT IS SENDING UPSTREAM.
 FINDINGS.md item 2 is `Math chapter Cordic` claiming ~0.1% and delivering 0.45%,
 with a test that never calls a Cordic function. The claim above was 17x out for
 the same reason -- it was reasoned about rather than run -- and it is corrected
 here rather than left standing while we mail someone else's.

  exp-poly : Real -> Real
  exp-poly (r) =
    let t1 = r
    in let t2 = t1 * r / 2.0
    in let t3 = t2 * r / 3.0
    in let t4 = t3 * r / 4.0
    in let t5 = t4 * r / 5.0
    in let t6 = t5 * r / 6.0
    in let t7 = t6 * r / 7.0
    in let t8 = t7 * r / 8.0
    in let t9 = t8 * r / 9.0
    in let t10 = t9 * r / 10.0
    in let t11 = t10 * r / 11.0
    in let t12 = t11 * r / 12.0
    in 1.0 + t1 + t2 + t3 + t4 + t5 + t6 + t7 + t8 + t9 + t10 + t11 + t12

 2^k by repeated multiplication rather than by assembling an exponent field: the
 bit-level route would need real-to-bits, which this arm still has no emitter for,
 and k is small enough here that the loop is exact anyway -- every step multiplies
 by a power of two, which is exact in binary until it underflows.

  pow2-up : Integer, Real -> Real
  pow2-up (k) (acc) = if k <= 0 then acc else pow2-up (k - 1) (acc * 2.0)

  pow2-down : Integer, Real -> Real
  pow2-down (k) (acc) = if k >= 0 then acc else pow2-down (k + 1) (acc * 0.5)

  pow2-int : Integer -> Real
  pow2-int (k) = if k >= 0 then pow2-up k 1.0 else pow2-down k 1.0

  exp-real : Real -> Real
  exp-real (x) =
    let k = real-to-int (round-real (x / ln2))
    in exp-poly (x - real-from-int k * ln2) * pow2-int k

 Codex's foreword has a floor and no ceiling, so this is the identity
 ceil x = -floor(-x) rather than a second rounding rule. It was Render's until
 2026-09-03, where it sliced the road strip; nothing about it is a road.

  ceil-real : Real -> Real
  ceil-real (x) = 0.0 - floor-real (0.0 - x)

Chapter: Blit
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Paint

 The blitter's own arithmetic, in Codex.

 THE ORACLE HERE IS JAVASCRIPT, not a game module, which makes this the second
 chapter in judge/ whose answer does not come from zig. `HISTORICAL_WASM_ROOT/
 blitter.js` -- the frozen original, which this project never edits -- owns a
 shading recipe and four visibility thresholds, numbers chosen by eye in the one
 file nothing in this project can run. Every other decision the game makes is
 inside a scene checksum; these were inside nothing at all.

 WHAT IS HERE AND WHAT IS NOT. Only the arithmetic. `ctx.fillStyle` wants a CSS
 string and the guest has no business knowing that, so the colour crosses as a
 packed integer and the string stays in the browser. The split is not a
 compromise -- it is the actual boundary, and the JS half is about six lines.

 THE JS NO LONGER COMPUTES THESE. `blit-expand` below runs the whole recipe over
 a finished frame and rewrites the commands, so what crosses the wire is already
 decided and `web/blitter.js` only paints.

 IT DOES THAT WITHOUT MOVING THE GRADED SEAM, which is the reason it is a second
 pass rather than a change to Render. `port/Paint.codex`'s returned `List DrawCmd`
 is what nine checks in judge/ compare against paint.zig, and paint.zig is the
 ORACLE this project exists to agree with. An expansion that runs AFTER that list
 is complete leaves every one of those comparisons looking at the same thing it
 looked at yesterday.

 We say:

Section: Colour

 A colour is 0xRRGGBB packed into an Integer, one byte per channel, exactly as it
 crosses the draw buffer.

  chan-r : Integer -> Integer
  chan-r (c) = bit-and (bit-shr c 16) 255

  chan-g : Integer -> Integer
  chan-g (c) = bit-and (bit-shr c 8) 255

  chan-b : Integer -> Integer
  chan-b (c) = bit-and c 255

  pack-rgb : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pack-rgb (r) (g) (b) = bit-or (bit-or (bit-shl r 16) (bit-shl g 8)) b

 Scale one channel and saturate at 255. Rounding is the game's `round-real`, which
 ties AWAY FROM ZERO where JavaScript's `Math.round` ties toward +Infinity. The two
 agree on every value this can be given -- a channel is 0..255 and a brightness
 factor is never negative -- and they disagree at -0.5, which is why the agreement
 is written down rather than assumed.

  shade-chan : Integer, Real -> Integer
  shade-chan (v) (f) =
    let scaled = real-to-int (round-real (real-from-int v * f))
    in if scaled > 255 then 255 else scaled

  shade-color : Integer, Real -> Integer
  shade-color (c) (f) =
    pack-rgb (shade-chan (chan-r c) f) (shade-chan (chan-g c) f) (shade-chan (chan-b c) f)

Section: Shading Across the Width

 Darken both edges of a polygon and lift its middle, the whole effect scaled by a
 strength the caller passes so the shading can fade with distance. This is a
 shading MODEL and it belongs beside the geometry that decides how far away a thing
 is, not in a renderer.

 IT IS ABOUT POLYGONS AND NOT ABOUT TREES. Safari uses it for tree crowns and the
 blitter's tag was called CROWN for that reason, which is exactly the kind of name
 that stops a renderer being reusable: a building face in a night city takes the
 same recipe. The mechanism is named here and the subject is not.

 The three stop offsets are 0, 0.5 and 1 and are the canvas's business; only the
 colours are computed here.

  shade-edge-darken : Real = 0.4
  shade-middle-lift : Real = 0.25

  width-shade-edge : Integer, Real -> Integer
  width-shade-edge (color) (strength) = shade-color color (1.0 - shade-edge-darken * strength)

  width-shade-middle : Integer, Real -> Integer
  width-shade-middle (color) (strength) = shade-color color (1.0 + shade-middle-lift * strength)

 The three colours in stop order, which is the shape the blitter wants.

  width-shade-stops : Integer, Real -> List Integer
  width-shade-stops (color) (strength) =
    [width-shade-edge color strength, width-shade-middle color strength, width-shade-edge color strength]

Section: Worth Drawing

 Below these a command is not worth emitting, let alone painting. They are the
 guest's decisions and always were: it computes the radius and the alpha, so it
 can as easily not write the command -- which would shrink the buffer as well as
 the renderer.

  min-disc-radius : Real = 0.5
  min-disc-alpha : Real = 0.02
  min-gradient-radius : Real = 0.5
  min-shade-width : Real = 1.0

  disc-visible : Real, Real -> Boolean
  disc-visible (r) (alpha) = r >= min-disc-radius and alpha >= min-disc-alpha

  radial-visible : Real -> Boolean
  radial-visible (r) = r >= min-gradient-radius

 A polygon narrower than a pixel gets a flat fill: the gradient has nowhere to go.

  too-narrow-to-shade : Real, Real -> Boolean
  too-narrow-to-shade (min-x) (max-x) = max-x - min-x < min-shade-width

Section: The Expansion

 Run the recipe over a finished frame. Every command the renderer produced goes in;
 what comes out is what the canvas can paint without deciding anything.

 THREE KINDS OF REWRITE, and they are the three kinds of decision the browser used
 to make. A command below a threshold is DROPPED, so the buffer shrinks as well as
 the renderer. A shaded polygon becomes either a flat fill or a span-shaded one,
 with its three colours and its span computed here. Everything else passes through
 untouched.

 THE NEW TAG IS 2, and 2 was free: it used to be an emoji glyph the browser font
 rasterised, and the critters have been baked to polygons since. It says a polygon
 is filled with a symmetric three-stop gradient across a span -- edge colour, middle
 colour, edge colour, at 0, 0.5 and 1 -- and it says nothing about why. The tag it
 replaces carried a `strength` and left the renderer holding a darkening factor, a
 lifting factor and a width floor; this one carries two colours and two x's.

  span-lo : List Real, Integer, Real -> Real
  span-lo (xs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length xs then acc
    else span-lo xs (i + 2) (if list-at xs i < acc then list-at xs i else acc)

  span-hi : List Real, Integer, Real -> Real
  span-hi (xs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length xs then acc
    else span-hi xs (i + 2) (if list-at xs i > acc then list-at xs i else acc)

 A shaded polygon with nothing to shade is a flat one, and it keeps its points.

  as-solid : DrawCmd -> DrawCmd
  as-solid (c) =
    DrawCmd { tag = 0, color = c.color, color2 = 0, strength = 0.0, geom = [], pts = c.pts }

  as-span-shade : DrawCmd, Real, Real -> DrawCmd
  as-span-shade (c) (lo) (hi) =
    DrawCmd {
      tag = 2,
      color = width-shade-edge c.color c.strength,
      color2 = width-shade-middle c.color c.strength,
      strength = 0.0,
      geom = [lo, hi],
      pts = c.pts
    }

 A polygon with fewer than two points has no extent to read, so it cannot be
 shaded. push-round-poly already refuses to emit one, and this says so rather than
 indexing an empty list to find out.

  expand-shade : DrawCmd -> DrawCmd
  expand-shade (c) =
    if c.strength <= 0.0 then as-solid c
    else if list-length c.pts < 2 then as-solid c
    else let lo = span-lo c.pts 0 (list-at c.pts 0)
      in let hi = span-hi c.pts 0 (list-at c.pts 0)
      in if too-narrow-to-shade lo hi then as-solid c else as-span-shade c lo hi

 A disc carries its radius in `geom` and its alpha in `strength`; a radial fill
 carries its radius third in `geom`, after the centre.

  expand-cmd : DrawCmd -> List DrawCmd
  expand-cmd (c) =
    if c.tag == 1 then [expand-shade c]
    else if c.tag == 3 then (if disc-visible (list-at c.geom 2) c.strength then [c] else [])
    else if c.tag == 4 then (if radial-visible (list-at c.geom 2) then [c] else [])
    else [c]

 THE INDEX IS A PARAMETER AND NOT A WRAPPER'S SECRET. A Codex definition whose body
 is a single application is inlined and never emitted, so a one-argument
 `blit-expand cs = expand-from cs 0` would not exist for the shim to call
 (PORTING_NOTES B12). The shim passes the 0.

  blit-expand : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  blit-expand (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else expand-cmd (list-at cs i) & blit-expand cs (i + 1)

Page 1

Chapter: Grade

 The reusable grader: compare a flattened list of values against gold and say
 WHERE the first disagreement is.

 Every seam in this port reduces to the same shape -- a list of Reals, a list of
 Integers, a list of Booleans -- so one grader serves pond, the camera, and later
 the draw-command buffer, which is itself a flat list. Flattening at the seam is
 what makes that true.

 The judge is built out of nothing it judges: g-abs duplicates real-abs on
 purpose. DeviceMath's own accuracy test records why -- when its judge called the
 chapter under test, collapsing that chapter left every cell reading ok.

 A failure names the INDEX, not just BAD. `show` on a Real is refused by the zig
 plug, which needs a __real_to_text it does not have, so a Real cannot be printed
 to say what it was; an Integer index can, and it points at one value instead of
 at a whole seam. When real-to-int lands, this is the chapter that grows a
 scaled-integer dump and the tolerance argument goes away.

 We say:

Section: Judging Reals

  g-abs : Real -> Real
  g-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

 A NaN USED TO PASS EVERY GATE IN THIS CHAPTER, silently, and it is worth saying
 exactly how. Each grader below asks "is |got - want| GREATER THAN the tolerance".
 Subtract a NaN and the difference is a NaN; `g-abs` of a NaN is a NaN, because
 `NaN < 0.0` is false and the else arm hands it back; and `NaN > tol` is false, so
 the value is not flagged and the walk moves on. Infinity was always caught --
 `inf - want` is `inf`, which does exceed any tolerance -- but a NaN is invisible
 to a comparison-based gate BY CONSTRUCTION, and there were tens of thousands of
 Reals a run passing through gates shaped exactly like this.

 IT WAS ALSO UNDETECTABLE UNTIL 2026-08-30. `show` on a Real is refused by the zig
 plug, and a NaN is the one value that does not equal itself, so no comparison and
 no printing available to this chapter could see one. `real-to-bits` is what
 changed: it landed in the zig plug that day (plugs-backlog 2.07) and a bit pattern
 does not care whether the value has an equal.

 THE TEST IS ONE MASK AND ONE COMPARISON. Clear the sign bit and every finite
 double is below #7FF0000000000000; positive infinity IS that number and every NaN
 is above it. So one gate catches both, and it is exact rather than approximate.

  g-finite : Real -> Boolean
  g-finite (x) = bit-and (real-to-bits x) #7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF < #7FF0000000000000

  first-real-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-real-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-finite (list-at got i) then
      (if g-abs (list-at got i - list-at want i) > tol then i
       else first-real-diff got want tol (i + 1))
    else i

  grade-reals : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-reals (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-real-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Reals Relatively

 A RELATIVE tolerance, scaled by the larger of 1 and the wanted magnitude -- the
 form DeviceMath's own accuracy test uses. Computed values need it: the port
 works in Real (f64) while the game computes in f32, so a projected pixel and a
 focal length disagree in proportion to their size, not by a fixed amount. An
 absolute tolerance that passes a 0.9 duck height would wave through a 4-pixel
 error on a 685-pixel focal.

  g-max : Real, Real -> Real
  g-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  first-rel-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-rel-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-finite (list-at got i) then
      (let w = list-at want i
       in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > tol * g-max 1.0 (g-abs w) then i
       else first-rel-diff got want tol (i + 1))
    else i

  grade-rel : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-rel (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-rel-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Cancelled Coordinates

 A MIXED tolerance: an absolute floor plus a relative term, which is the only form
 that fits a value produced by cancellation. Neither half works alone, and both
 failures were measured rather than guessed.

 IT IS NAMED FOR THE FAILURE MODE, NOT THE UNIT, and `grade-px` keeps its name
 because screen pixels were the first seam to need it and most of its callers are
 still projections. RenderCheck grades rider-frame METRES with it: `at` composes a
 point down a kilometre of chain and returns one a metre or two off the rider's
 nose, which is the same small-difference-of-two-large-numbers this gate exists
 for, in a different unit. The FLOOR is what changes with the unit -- a thousandth
 of a pixel on a canvas, an f32 ulp at the range a chain reaches -- and D5's rule
 is that it is calibrated per seam and never inherited.

 A RELATIVE gate fails near the horizon. A screen y is 300 minus a term of about
 the same size, so a y of 20.6 is a small difference of two large numbers and
 cancellation amplifies f32's relative error by the ratio between them -- fifteen
 times epsilon on the first cone tier of a near tree.

 An ABSOLUTE gate fails far off screen. Projecting a point 37 m to the side at the
 0.4 m near plane puts x at about -62929, where one f32 ulp is already 0.0075 px --
 seven times a thousandth-of-a-pixel floor, before any port is involved.

 The floor governs coordinates near zero and on the canvas; the relative term
 governs the far-off-screen ones the near plane throws out. At a hundred pixels the
 gate is about a thousandth of a pixel, which is the precision worth asserting.

  grade-px : Text, List Real, List Real, Real, Real -> Text
  grade-px (name) (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-px-diff got want atol rtol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

  first-px-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-px-diff (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-finite (list-at got i) then
      (let w = list-at want i
       in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > atol + rtol * g-abs w then i
       else first-px-diff got want atol rtol (i + 1))
    else i

Section: Judging Integers

  first-int-diff : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  first-int-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if list-at got i /= list-at want i then i
    else first-int-diff got want (i + 1)

  grade-ints : Text, List Integer, List Integer -> Text
  grade-ints (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-int-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i & " got " & show (list-at got i) & " want " & show (list-at want i)

Section: Judging Booleans

  bool-eq : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  bool-eq (a) (b) = if a then b else if b then False else True

  first-bool-diff : List Boolean, List Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  first-bool-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if bool-eq (list-at got i) (list-at want i) then first-bool-diff got want (i + 1)
    else i

  grade-bools : Text, List Boolean, List Boolean -> Text
  grade-bools (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-bool-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Chapter: BlitSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Blit
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Blit: the pass that runs over a finished frame and turns what the renderer
 produced into what the browser receives.

 IT IS THE ONLY PLACE TAG 2 EXISTS. Paint emits tag 1, a polygon with a shading
 strength; this pass reads the polygon's x-extent and rewrites it as either a
 flat tag 0 or a three-stop tag 2 with the colours already computed. So a reader
 of `web/blitter.js` finds no tag 1 and a reader of `Safari chapter Paint` finds
 no tag 2, and this chapter is the hinge between them.

 THREE WAYS A SHADE BECOMES FLAT, and they are separate branches that a rewrite
 would happily merge: no strength at all, too few points to have an extent, and
 an extent under a pixel. All three keep the polygon and its colour untouched --
 the command is downgraded, never dropped -- and each is graded, because merging
 them into one test would still pass any check that only asked for tag 0.

 THE DROPS ARE A DIFFERENT MATTER AND GO THE OTHER WAY. A disc under half a pixel
 or under two per cent alpha, and a radial fill under half a pixel, are REMOVED
 from the frame. Both thresholds are `>=`, so a command exactly on one survives,
 and that boundary is graded from both sides.

 EVERY COLOUR HERE IS AN INTEGER, so the shading ladder is exact: 0x804020 has
 channels 128, 64 and 32, which scale to whole numbers at the edge factor and the
 middle factor alike. The saturation case is the one that needs a wide colour --
 250 lifted by a quarter is 312.5, which rounds to 313 and clamps.

 We say:

Section: Channels

 A colour is 0xRRGGBB and the three accessors are shifts and masks, so packing
 what was unpacked gives the colour back.

  chan-got : List Integer
  chan-got =
    [ chan-r 8405024, chan-g 8405024, chan-b 8405024,
      pack-rgb (chan-r 8405024) (chan-g 8405024) (chan-b 8405024),
      chan-r 16777215, chan-g 16777215, chan-b 16777215,
      chan-r 0, pack-rgb 0 0 0, pack-rgb 255 255 255 ]

  chan-want : List Integer
  chan-want = [128, 64, 32, 8405024, 255, 255, 255, 0, 0, 16777215]

 Scaling a channel rounds and then saturates -- it never wraps, which is the
 failure a mask instead of a clamp would give.

  shade-got : List Integer
  shade-got =
    [ shade-chan 128 0.6, shade-chan 64 0.6, shade-chan 32 0.6,
      shade-chan 250 1.25, shade-chan 255 1.0, shade-chan 255 2.0,
      shade-chan 0 1.25, shade-color 8405024 0.6 ]

  shade-want : List Integer
  shade-want = [77, 38, 19, 255, 255, 255, 0, 5056019]

Section: The Three Stops

 The edges darken by four tenths of the strength and the middle lifts by a
 quarter of it, so the stops come out dark, bright, dark -- and the first and
 third are the SAME colour, which is what makes the gradient symmetric.

  stops-got : List Integer
  stops-got =
    width-shade-stops 8405024 1.0 & width-shade-stops 8405024 0.5
      & [width-shade-edge 8405024 1.0, width-shade-middle 8405024 1.0]

  stops-want : List Integer
  stops-want =
    [ 5056019, 10506280, 5056019,
      6697754, 9455652, 6697754,
      5056019, 10506280 ]

 At zero strength both factors are one, so every stop is the colour itself.

  zero-got : List Integer
  zero-got = width-shade-stops 8405024 0.0

  zero-want : List Integer
  zero-want = [8405024, 8405024, 8405024]

  glow-got : List Integer
  glow-got = [shade-chan 250 1.25, width-shade-middle 16448250 1.0]

  glow-want : List Integer
  glow-want = [255, 16777215]

Section: Worth Drawing

 Both thresholds are `>=`, so a command sitting exactly on one is kept.

  visible-got : List Boolean
  visible-got =
    [ disc-visible 0.5 0.02, disc-visible 0.49 0.02, disc-visible 0.5 0.019,
      disc-visible 10.0 1.0, radial-visible 0.5, radial-visible 0.49,
      too-narrow-to-shade 10.0 10.5, too-narrow-to-shade 10.0 11.0, too-narrow-to-shade 10.0 30.0 ]

  visible-want : List Boolean
  visible-want = [True, False, False, True, True, False, True, False, False]

  limits-got : List Real
  limits-got = [min-disc-radius, min-disc-alpha, min-gradient-radius, min-shade-width, shade-edge-darken, shade-middle-lift]

  limits-want : List Real
  limits-want = [0.5, 0.02, 0.5, 1.0, 0.4, 0.25]

Section: The Extent

 The x coordinates are the even entries of the flattened polygon, and the walk
 skips the y's.

  poly : List Real
  poly = [10.0, 1.0, 30.0, 2.0, 20.0, 3.0]

  span-got : List Real
  span-got =
    [ span-lo poly 0 (list-at poly 0), span-hi poly 0 (list-at poly 0),
      span-lo [5.0, 99.0] 0 5.0, span-hi [5.0, 99.0] 0 5.0 ]

  span-want : List Real
  span-want = [10.0, 30.0, 5.0, 5.0]

Section: The Rewrite

  cmd : Integer, Integer, Real, List Real, List Real -> DrawCmd
  cmd (tag) (color) (strength) (geom) (pts) =
    DrawCmd { tag = tag, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = strength, geom = geom, pts = pts }

 A wide shaded polygon becomes tag 2: the edge colour first, the middle second,
 the extent in `geom`, and the polygon carried through unchanged.

  wide : DrawCmd
  wide = expand-shade (cmd 1 8405024 1.0 [] poly)

  wide-got : List Integer
  wide-got = [wide.tag, wide.color, wide.color2, list-length wide.geom, list-length wide.pts]

  wide-want : List Integer
  wide-want = [2, 5056019, 10506280, 2, 6]

  wide-geom-got : List Real
  wide-geom-got = wide.geom & wide.pts & [wide.strength]

  wide-geom-want : List Real
  wide-geom-want = [10.0, 30.0, 10.0, 1.0, 30.0, 2.0, 20.0, 3.0, 0.0]

 The three ways it stays flat. Each keeps the colour and the points and answers
 tag 0 with an empty `geom`.

  flat-of : DrawCmd -> List Integer
  flat-of (c) =
    let e = expand-shade c
    in [e.tag, e.color, e.color2, list-length e.geom, list-length e.pts]

  flat-got : List Integer
  flat-got =
    flat-of (cmd 1 8405024 0.0 [] poly)
      & flat-of (cmd 1 8405024 1.0 [] [5.0])
      & flat-of (cmd 1 8405024 1.0 [] [10.0, 1.0, 10.5, 2.0, 10.2, 3.0])

  flat-want : List Integer
  flat-want =
    [ 0, 8405024, 0, 0, 6,
      0, 8405024, 0, 0, 1,
      0, 8405024, 0, 0, 6 ]

Section: The Frame

 Everything that is not a tag 1, a disc or a radial fill passes through
 untouched, and the two that can vanish do so by leaving the list shorter.

  frame : List DrawCmd
  frame =
    [ cmd 0 111 0.0 [] poly,
      cmd 1 8405024 1.0 [] poly,
      cmd 3 222 0.02 [1.0, 2.0, 0.5] [],
      cmd 3 333 0.019 [1.0, 2.0, 0.5] [],
      cmd 3 444 1.0 [1.0, 2.0, 0.49] [],
      cmd 4 555 0.0 [1.0, 2.0, 0.5] poly,
      cmd 4 666 0.0 [1.0, 2.0, 0.49] poly,
      cmd 5 777 0.0 [] poly ]

  frame-got : List Integer
  frame-got =
    let out = blit-expand frame 0
    in [ list-length out,
         (list-at out 0).tag, (list-at out 0).color,
         (list-at out 1).tag, (list-at out 1).color,
         (list-at out 2).tag, (list-at out 2).color,
         (list-at out 3).tag, (list-at out 3).color,
         (list-at out 4).tag, (list-at out 4).color ]

  frame-want : List Integer
  frame-want = [5, 0, 111, 2, 5056019, 3, 222, 4, 555, 5, 777]

  empty-got : List Integer
  empty-got = [list-length (blit-expand [] 0), list-length (expand-cmd (cmd 3 1 0.5 [0.0, 0.0, 0.1] []))]

  empty-want : List Integer
  empty-want = [0, 0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-chan  " chan-got chan-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-shade " shade-got shade-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-stops " stops-got stops-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-zero  " zero-got zero-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-glow  " glow-got glow-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "bl-vis   " visible-got visible-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "bl-limits" limits-got limits-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "bl-span  " span-got span-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-wide  " wide-got wide-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "bl-wgeom " wide-geom-got wide-geom-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-flat  " flat-got flat-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-frame " frame-got frame-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "bl-empty " empty-got empty-want)
  end
